@*
    What a civic costs in content packs, and whether what you own suits.

    A tick or a cross rather than a colour, and for the same reason the conditions carry them: the
    game writes both directions and one of them is easy to read backwards. Corporate Dominion needs
    you to NOT have Megacorp - it is the civic for an oligarchy that cannot be one - and a chip that
    simply said "Megacorp" told the reader to buy the pack that takes it away.

    The mark sits outside the chip, where the conditions put theirs. Inside, it was a mark on the
    pack rather than a mark on the requirement, and the one control on the page whose parts were
    arranged differently from every other.

    Whether the gate is satisfied is said by the colour and by the tip. That is a different fact
    from which way the gate runs, and both are worth having: a pack you do not own is something you
    could buy, and a pack you own that rules a civic out is not a problem to solve at all.
*@

@foreach (var pack in Packs)
{
    <span class="sem-pack-gate @(pack.Satisfied ? null : "unmet")" title="@Told(pack)">
        <span class="mark @(pack.Wanted ? "wants" : "refuses")" aria-hidden="true">
            @(pack.Wanted ? "✓" : "✗")
        </span>

        <span class="sem-visually-hidden">@(pack.Wanted ? "Needs" : "Must not have")</span>

        <span class="sem-cell-chip">
            @if (pack.Icon is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <GameIcon Path="@pack.Icon" Alt="" />
            }

            <span>@pack.Name</span>
        </span>
    </span>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The packs this civic depends on, in either direction.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<WikiPack> Packs { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole of what one chip means, which is more than two words can carry.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Four sentences for four states, because every pair of them differs in something a reader
    /// would act on. Needing a pack you lack is a thing to buy; being ruled out by one you own is a
    /// thing to know and nothing to do about.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Told(WikiPack pack) => (pack.Wanted, pack.Held) switch
    {
        (true, true) => $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you have.",
        (true, false) => $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you do not have.",
        (false, false) => $"Only without {pack.Name}, which you do not have.",
        _ => $"Only without {pack.Name}, and you have it.",
    };
}
